OSKit INET TCP — setsockopt / getsockopt helpers
================================================

Same-module sibling of `tcp.lsysl`. Owns the small but talkative
cluster of `set_*` / `get_*` helpers that back the SO_SNDBUF /
SO_RCVBUF / SO_KEEPALIVE / TCP_KEEPIDLE / TCP_KEEPINTVL /
TCP_KEEPCNT / SLIX_TCP_ACK_BLACKHOLE / SLIX_TCP_USERTO sockopt
ABIs:

* `inet_tcp_resize_buf` — SO_SNDBUF / SO_RCVBUF
* `inet_tcp_set_keepalive` — SO_KEEPALIVE on/off
* `inet_tcp_set_userto` — RFC 5482 TCP_USER_TIMEOUT
* `inet_tcp_set_ack_blackhole` — test-only ACK suppression
* `inet_tcp_set_keepopt` / `inet_tcp_get_keepopt` — keepalive tunables
* `inet_tcp_eff_keepalive` — effective values (overrides + defaults)
* `inet_tcp_get_bufsize` — getsockopt(SO_SNDBUF/SO_RCVBUF)

Sockopts live together because they share validation patterns
(slot ownership, state guards, the [TCP_BUF_MIN, TCP_BUF_MAX]
clamp) and have no callers outside the command-handler layer
in `inet_tcp_handlers.lsysl`.

    module oskit.servers

    import oskit.services.{monotonic_ms}
    import std.alloc.{free, malloc}


setsockopt(SO_SNDBUF) / setsockopt(SO_RCVBUF) implementation. Resizes
the heap-backed send or recv buffer on socket `s`. `kind` selects
which buffer (0 = SNDBUF, 1 = RCVBUF). `size` is the requested new
size in bytes (clamped to [TCP_BUF_MIN, TCP_BUF_MAX]).

Resize only happens in the CLOSED state with no buffered data. After
connect/listen/accept, Linux silently accepts the call but ignores
the new value (`tcp_set_rcvbuf` warns at most once); we mirror that
"return 0, no-op" behavior so well-behaved servers that call
`setsockopt` after `accept` don't get spurious EINVALs. Returns 0 on
success (whether or not a resize actually happened), -1 on bad fd or
out-of-range kind.

    inet_tcp_resize_buf(s: int, kind: int, size: int) -> int
        if s < 0 || s >= TCP_MAX_SOCKETS
            return -1
        if tcp_sockets[s].in_use == 0
            return -1
        if kind != 0 && kind != 1
            return -1
        var clamped = size
        if clamped < TCP_BUF_MIN
            clamped = TCP_BUF_MIN
        if clamped > TCP_BUF_MAX
            clamped = TCP_BUF_MAX
        // Phase 2 chunk 2 — per-tid TCP buffer-memory cap.  Linux-
        // ish silent clamp: if growing the buffer would push owner
        // over TCP_PER_TID_BUF_MAX, shrink the request to whatever
        // budget remains. owners_count == 0 means alloc has run but
        // begin_*/handle_passive_syn hasn't stamped yet — skip the
        // check; the caller will own the budget after stamping.
        val owner = inet_tcp_owners_primary(s)
        if owner >= 0
            var current = tcp_sockets[s].send_buf_size
            if kind != 0
                current = tcp_sockets[s].recv_buf_size
            if clamped > current
                val others = inet_tcp_buf_bytes_owner(owner) - current
                val budget = TCP_PER_TID_BUF_MAX - others
                if clamped > budget
                    clamped = budget
                if clamped < TCP_BUF_MIN
                    return 0
        // Only the CLOSED state with empty buffers is a safe resize
        // window: connect/listen latch the buffer pointer into flow-
        // control state, and copying live in-flight data on a
        // realloc would race the receive path.
        if tcp_sockets[s].state != TCP_STATE_CLOSED
            return 0
        if kind == 0
            if tcp_sockets[s].send_len != 0
                return 0
            if clamped == tcp_sockets[s].send_buf_size
                return 0
            free(tcp_sockets[s].send_buf_ptr)
            tcp_sockets[s].send_buf_ptr = malloc(i64(clamped))
            tcp_sockets[s].send_buf_size = clamped
        else
            if tcp_sockets[s].recv_len != 0
                return 0
            if clamped == tcp_sockets[s].recv_buf_size
                return 0
            free(tcp_sockets[s].recv_buf_ptr)
            tcp_sockets[s].recv_buf_ptr = malloc(i64(clamped))
            tcp_sockets[s].recv_buf_size = clamped
        0


setsockopt(SO_KEEPALIVE) — flips the per-socket keepalive flag.
Turning it on baselines `last_recv_ms` to now and clears any
in-flight probe count, so a freshly-enabled socket waits a full
`TCP_KEEPALIVE_IDLE_MS` from this point before the first probe.
Turning it off zeroes the schedule.

Idempotent (no-op for same-state calls).  Returns 0 on success,
-1 on bad fd / closed slot.

    inet_tcp_set_keepalive(s: int, on: int) -> int
        if s < 0 || s >= TCP_MAX_SOCKETS
            return -1
        if tcp_sockets[s].in_use == 0
            return -1
        if on != 0
            tcp_sockets[s].keepalive_on = 1
            tcp_sockets[s].last_recv_ms = int(monotonic_ms())
            tcp_sockets[s].keepalive_probes = 0
            tcp_sockets[s].keepalive_next_probe = 0
        else
            tcp_sockets[s].keepalive_on = 0
            tcp_sockets[s].keepalive_probes = 0
            tcp_sockets[s].keepalive_next_probe = 0
        0


setsockopt(TCP_USER_TIMEOUT) — write the per-socket cap in
milliseconds (the unit the user already passed at the POSIX
boundary; RFC 5482 §3 is in ms, the man page is misleading).
0 disables (Linux's "system default" semantics — slix has no
sysctl for this, so 0 means "no cap").

Returns 0 on success, -1 on bad fd.

    inet_tcp_set_userto(s: int, timeout_ms: int) -> int
        if s < 0 || s >= TCP_MAX_SOCKETS
            return -1
        if tcp_sockets[s].in_use == 0
            return -1
        var t = timeout_ms
        if t < 0
            t = 0
        tcp_sockets[s].user_timeout_ms = t
        0


Test-only: arm or disarm the ACK blackhole on a slot.  When set,
`inet_tcp_emit` drops pure-ACK segments from this slot, so the
peer never sees acknowledgements while SYN/FIN/RST handshake
flow continues.  Used by `slix/test/musrto.c` to drive a
TCP_USER_TIMEOUT abort over the loopback fastpath, which would
otherwise ACK every segment synchronously.

Returns 0 on success, -1 on bad fd.

    inet_tcp_set_ack_blackhole(s: int, on: int) -> int
        if s < 0 || s >= TCP_MAX_SOCKETS
            return -1
        if tcp_sockets[s].in_use == 0
            return -1
        if on != 0
            tcp_sockets[s].ack_blackholed = 1
        else
            tcp_sockets[s].ack_blackholed = 0
        0


setsockopt(TCP_KEEPIDLE / TCP_KEEPINTVL / TCP_KEEPCNT) — write a
per-socket override that scan_timers consults instead of the
global default. `kind` selects which option (0 = idle, 1 = intvl,
2 = cnt). Idle/intvl arrive in *milliseconds* (the shim has already
converted from seconds via `secs * 1000`); cnt is a raw probe count.
Clamp to a non-zero positive — 0 is reserved internally to mean
"use default", so a caller passing 0 still routes to the global
constant. Resets the probe schedule so the new cadence takes
effect on the next scan, regardless of any in-flight probe.

Returns 0 on success, -1 on bad fd / unknown kind.

    inet_tcp_set_keepopt(s: int, kind: int, value: int) -> int
        if s < 0 || s >= TCP_MAX_SOCKETS
            return -1
        if tcp_sockets[s].in_use == 0
            return -1
        var v = value
        if v < 0
            v = 0
        if kind == 0
            tcp_sockets[s].keepalive_idle = v
        else if kind == 1
            tcp_sockets[s].keepalive_intvl = v
        else if kind == 2
            tcp_sockets[s].keepalive_cnt = v
        else
            return -1
        // Force a reschedule on the next scan: any cached probe
        // deadline was derived from the old cadence and would
        // misbehave (probe too soon / too late) under the new
        // values.
        tcp_sockets[s].keepalive_next_probe = 0
        0


getsockopt(TCP_KEEPIDLE / TCP_KEEPINTVL / TCP_KEEPCNT) — read the
persisted per-socket override. Returns the *milliseconds* / count.
The caller (typically the shim) is responsible for converting back
to seconds. Returns -1 on bad fd / unknown kind. A 0 reply means
"using the global default" — distinct from a real 0, which is
forbidden by the setter clamp.

    inet_tcp_get_keepopt(s: int, kind: int) -> int
        if s < 0 || s >= TCP_MAX_SOCKETS
            return -1
        if tcp_sockets[s].in_use == 0
            return -1
        if kind == 0
            return tcp_sockets[s].keepalive_idle
        if kind == 1
            return tcp_sockets[s].keepalive_intvl
        if kind == 2
            return tcp_sockets[s].keepalive_cnt
        -1


Resolve the effective keepalive timing for socket `s`. Returns
the values scan_timers should use, falling back to the global
default whenever a per-socket override is 0. Centralized so the
fallback rule stays in one place rather than duplicated three
times in scan_timers.

    inet_tcp_eff_keepalive(s: int, out idle: int, out intvl: int, out cnt: int)
        val raw_idle = tcp_sockets[s].keepalive_idle
        val raw_intvl = tcp_sockets[s].keepalive_intvl
        val raw_cnt = tcp_sockets[s].keepalive_cnt
        idle = if raw_idle != 0 then raw_idle else TCP_KEEPALIVE_IDLE_MS
        intvl = if raw_intvl != 0 then raw_intvl else TCP_KEEPALIVE_INTVL_MS
        cnt = if raw_cnt != 0 then raw_cnt else TCP_KEEPALIVE_MAX_PROBES


    inet_tcp_get_bufsize(s: int, kind: int) -> int
        if s < 0 || s >= TCP_MAX_SOCKETS
            return -1
        if tcp_sockets[s].in_use == 0
            return -1
        if kind == 0
            return tcp_sockets[s].send_buf_size
        if kind == 1
            return tcp_sockets[s].recv_buf_size
        -1
